.703125)\" xlink:href=\"#STIXGeneral-Italic-107\"></use>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n  </g>\n </g>\n <defs>\n  <clipPath id=\"p98bed16193\">\n   <rect height=\"166.32\" width=\"167.4\" x=\"7.2\" y=\"13.271932\"></rect>\n  </clipPath>\n </defs>\n</svg>\n<div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for diagram of 3 lines\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul>\n<li>Clockwise from top left, the 3 lines are labeled t, j, and k.</li>\n<li>Line t intersects both line j and line k.</li>\n<li>At the intersection of line t and line j, 2 angles are labeled clockwise from top left as follows:<br>\n<ul>\n<li>Bottom right: 133°</li>\n<li>Bottom left: x°</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the figure, line <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> is parallel to line <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>. What is the value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>?</p>","externalid":"823ebf16-5273-4421-aeea-7ddb20cca4fd","templateid":"e04afc7a-a0fe-406d-85a8-baf184b3223a","vaultid":"c010633d-dbee-4002-89a3-6c6614ef8f9c","type":"spr","answerOptions":[],"correct_answer":["47"]},"createDate":1691007959838,"updateDate":1691007959838}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959838, 1691007959838),
    ($SATQ$3b931fb0$SATQ$, $SATQ$9f8442fc-4e43-48a4-9eda-fd446fd06043$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A right circular cylinder has a volume of <math alttext="377"><mn>377</mn>
</math> cubic centimeters. The area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext="13"><mn>13</mn>
</math> square centimeters. What is the height, in centimeters, of the cylinder?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["29"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["29"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="29"><mn>29</mn>
</math>. The volume, <math alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi>
</math>, of a right circular cylinder is given by the formula <math alttext="upper V equals pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math> is the radius of the base of the cylinder and <math alttext="h"><mi>h</mi>
</math> is the height of the cylinder. Since the base of the cylinder is a circle with radius <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math>, the area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext="pi r squared"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. It's given that a right circular cylinder has a volume of <math alttext="377"><mn>377</mn>
</math> cubic centimeters; therefore, <math alttext="upper V equals 377"><mrow>
	<mi>V</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>377</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. It's also given that the area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext="13"><mn>13</mn>
</math> square centimeters; therefore, <math alttext="pi r squared equals 13"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math>. Substituting <math alttext="377"><mn>377</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="13"><mn>13</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="pi r squared"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> in the formula&nbsp;<math alttext="upper V equals pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math alttext="377 equals 13 h"><mrow>
	<mn>377</mn>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>13</mn>
		<mi>h</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="13"><mn>13</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="29 equals h"><mrow>
	<mn>29</mn>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mi>h</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the height of the cylinder, in centimeters, is <math alttext="29"><mn>29</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3b931fb0","external_id":"9f8442fc-4e43-48a4-9eda-fd446fd06043","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"232a848c-db72-4553-ba91-72a9d3411796","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A right circular cylinder has a volume of <math alttext=\"377\"><mn>377</mn>\n</math> cubic centimeters. The area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> square centimeters. What is the height, in centimeters, of the cylinder?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["29"],"correct_answer":["29"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"29\"><mn>29</mn>\n</math>. The volume, <math alttext=\"upper V\"><mi>V</mi>\n</math>, of a right circular cylinder is given by the formula <math alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi>\n</math> is the radius of the base of the cylinder and <math alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi>\n</math> is the height of the cylinder. Since the base of the cylinder is a circle with radius <math alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi>\n</math>, the area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext=\"pi r squared\"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. It's given that a right circular cylinder has a volume of <math alttext=\"377\"><mn>377</mn>\n</math> cubic centimeters; therefore, <math alttext=\"upper V equals 377\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>V</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>377</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It's also given that the area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> square centimeters; therefore, <math alttext=\"pi r squared equals 13\"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"377\"><mn>377</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper V\"><mi>V</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"pi r squared\"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> in the formula&nbsp;<math alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math alttext=\"377 equals 13 h\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>377</mn>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>13</mn>\n\t\t<mi>h</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"29 equals h\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mi>h</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, the height of the cylinder, in centimeters, is <math alttext=\"29\"><mn>29</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357406,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"3b931fb0","skill_cd":"S.A.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Area and volume","createDate":1730147357406,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"9f8442fc-4e43-48a4-9eda-fd446fd06043","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"232a848c-db72-4553-ba91-72a9d3411796","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"answerOptions":[],"externalid":"9f8442fc-4e43-48a4-9eda-fd446fd06043","keys":["29"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"29\"><mn>29</mn>\n</math>. The volume, <math alttext=\"upper V\"><mi>V</mi>\n</math>, of a right circular cylinder is given by the formula <math alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi>\n</math> is the radius of the base of the cylinder and <math alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi>\n</math> is the height of the cylinder. Since the base of the cylinder is a circle with radius <math alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi>\n</math>, the area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext=\"pi r squared\"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. It's given that a right circular cylinder has a volume of <math alttext=\"377\"><mn>377</mn>\n</math> cubic centimeters; therefore, <math alttext=\"upper V equals 377\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>V</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>377</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It's also given that the area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> square centimeters; therefore, <math alttext=\"pi r squared equals 13\"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"377\"><mn>377</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper V\"><mi>V</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"pi r squared\"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> in the formula&nbsp;<math alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math alttext=\"377 equals 13 h\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>377</mn>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>13</mn>\n\t\t<mi>h</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"29 equals h\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mi>h</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, the height of the cylinder, in centimeters, is <math alttext=\"29\"><mn>29</mn>\n</math>.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A right circular cylinder has a volume of <math alttext=\"377\"><mn>377</mn>\n</math> cubic centimeters. The area of the base of the cylinder is <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> square centimeters. What is the height, in centimeters, of the cylinder?</p>","type":"spr","correct_answer":["29"]},"createDate":1730147357406,"updateDate":1730147357406}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357406, 1730147357406),
    ($SATQ$3e577e4a$SATQ$, $SATQ$bf1e177f-7c4f-4d1a-9d5d-36f0191c1cde$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$330929ad-f93b-44d8-89cb-e09e3444645e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Circles$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane has its center at <math alttext="left parenthesis negative 4 comma negative 6 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></mfenced></math>. Line <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math> is tangent to this circle at the point <math alttext="left parenthesis negative 7 comma negative 7 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow></mfenced></math>. What is the slope of line <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0ec2c263-f331-4ecc-8c9c-6d2d1aab4106","content":"<p><math alttext=\"negative 3\"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d0bb724b-9eb2-452d-b4e9-bd6bab379b20","content":"<p><math alttext=\"negative one third\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"c2c504de-3966-474c-b3e4-2f9eec1d3dba","content":"<p><math alttext=\"one third\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e415b50e-7a38-43fa-9419-1c5ac58edc54","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0ec2c263-f331-4ecc-8c9c-6d2d1aab4106"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is correct. A line that's tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius of the circle at the point of tangency. It's given that the circle has its center at <math alttext="left parenthesis negative 4 comma negative 6 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> and line <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math> is tangent to the circle at the point <math alttext="left parenthesis negative 7 comma negative 7 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. The slope of a radius defined by the points <math alttext="left parenthesis q comma r right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math alttext="left parenthesis s comma t right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> can be calculated as <math alttext="StartFraction t minus r Over s minus q EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. The points <math alttext="left parenthesis negative 7 comma negative 7 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math alttext="left parenthesis negative 4 comma negative 6 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> define the radius of the circle at the point of tangency. Therefore, the slope of this radius can be calculated as <math alttext="StartFraction left parenthesis negative 6 right parenthesis minus left parenthesis negative 7 right parenthesis Over left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis minus left parenthesis negative 7 right parenthesis EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="one third"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. If a line and a radius are perpendicular, the slope of the line must be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the radius. The negative reciprocal of <math alttext="one third"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> is <math alttext="negative 3"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></math>. Thus, the slope of line <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math> is&nbsp;<math alttext="negative 3"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the slope of the radius of the circle at the point of tangency, not the slope of line <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math>.</p>
