 8 times the square root of 3 centimeters squared\"></span>.<p>Alternate approach: The area of a trapezoid can be found by evaluating the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_014_b2df9fce.png\" alt=\"one half times, open parenthesis, b sub 1 plus b sub 2, close parenthesis, times h\"></span>, where <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_015_db5ea6cf.png\" alt=\"b sub 1\"></span>is the length of one base, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_016_ff5faadd.png\" alt=\"b sub 2 \"></span> is the length of the other base, and <span class=\"italic\">h</span> is the height of the trapezoid. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_017_06200482.png\" alt=\"b sub 1 equals 8\"></span>, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_018_5de8928b.png\" alt=\"b sub 2 equals 4\"></span>, and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_019_ca5cd3c6.png\" alt=\"h equals, 2 times the square root of 3\"></span> yields the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_020_8e288b34.png\" alt=\"one half times, open parenthesis, 8 plus 4, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, 2 times the square root of 3, close parenthesis\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_021_0b2491cd.png\" alt=\"one half times 12, times, open parenthesis, 2 times the square root of 3, close parenthesis\"></span>, which gives an area of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_022_e7e304db.png\" alt=\"12 times the square root of 3 centimeters squared\"></span> for the trapezoid. Since two-thirds of the trapezoid is shaded, the area of the shaded region is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_023_acd2ea2a.png\" alt=\"two thirds times, 12 times the square root of 3, equals, 8 times the square root of 3\"></span>.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the height of the trapezoid. Choice B is incorrect. This is the area of one of the equilateral triangles, not two. Choice D is incorrect and may result from using a height of 4 for each triangle rather than the height of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/4c95c7d4/img_024_11e0544e.png\" alt=\"2 times the square root of 3\"></span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":25,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959634,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"4c95c7d4","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1691007959634,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"07822-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"4237133f-815d-412b-9d78-b85126aff976","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"07822-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \"><div class=\"standalone_image \"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"The figure presents a trapezoid, which consists of three congruent equilateral triangles. Two of the triangles are shaded, and each of those triangles shares a side with the unshaded triangle.\" width=\"124\" height=\"54\"></div></div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A graphic designer is creating a logo for a company. The logo is shown in the figure above. The logo is&nbsp;in&nbsp;the shape of a trapezoid and consists of three&nbsp;congruent equilateral triangles. If the perimeter of the logo is 20&nbsp;centimeters, what is the combined area of the shaded regions, in square centimeters, of the logo?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times the square root of 3\"></span></span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"4 times the square root of 3\"></span></span></p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"8 times the square root of 3\"></span></span></p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p>16</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"c","rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the logo is in the shape of a trapezoid that consists of three congruent equilateral triangles, and that the perimeter of the trapezoid is 20 centimeters (cm). Since the perimeter of the trapezoid is the sum of the lengths of 5 of the sides of the triangles, the length of each side of an equilateral triangle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 20 over 5 equals 4 centimeters\"></span>. Dividing up one equilateral triangle into two right triangles yields a pair of congruent 30&deg;-60&deg;-90&deg; triangles. The shorter leg of each right triangle is half the length of the side of an equilateral triangle, or 2 cm. Using the Pythagorean Theorem, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, squared, plus b squared, equals c squared\"></span>, the height of the equilateral triangle can be found. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, equals 2\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"c equals 4 \"></span> and solving for <span class=\"italic\">b</span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of, 4 squared, minus 2 squared, end root, equals the square root of 12, which equals, 2 times the square root of 3 centimeters\"></span> cm. The area of one equilateral triangle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one half b h\"></span>, where <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b equals 2 \"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"h equals, 2 times the square root of 3\"></span>. Therefore, the area of one equilateral triangle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one half times 4, times, open parenthesis, 2 times the square root of 3, close parenthesis, equals, 4 times the square root of 3 centimeters squared\"></span>. The shaded area consists of two such triangles, so its area is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times 4, times the square root of 3, equals, 8 times the square root of 3 centimeters squared\"></span>.<p>Alternate approach: The area of a trapezoid can be found by evaluating the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one half times, open parenthesis, b sub 1 plus b sub 2, close parenthesis, times h\"></span>, where <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b sub 1\"></span>is the length of one base, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b sub 2 \"></span> is the length of the other base, and <span class=\"italic\">h</span> is the height of the trapezoid. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b sub 1 equals 8\"></span>, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b sub 2 equals 4\"></span>, and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"h equals, 2 times the square root of 3\"></span> yields the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one half times, open parenthesis, 8 plus 4, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, 2 times the square root of 3, close parenthesis\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one half times 12, times, open parenthesis, 2 times the square root of 3, close parenthesis\"></span>, which gives an area of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"12 times the square root of 3 centimeters squared\"></span> for the trapezoid. Since two-thirds of the trapezoid is shaded, the area of the shaded region is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"two thirds times, 12 times the square root of 3, equals, 8 times the square root of 3\"></span>.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the height of the trapezoid. Choice B is incorrect. This is the area of one of the equilateral triangles, not two. Choice D is incorrect and may result from using a height of 4 for each triangle rather than the height of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times the square root of 3\"></span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959634,"updateDate":1691007959634}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959634, 1691007959634),
    ($SATQ$4efea6a3$SATQ$, $SATQ$2bde9f21-d49c-42af-b7ec-38c87b242d02$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The area of a rectangle is <math alttext="57"><mn>57</mn>
</math> square inches. The length of the longest side of the rectangle is <math alttext="19"><mn>19</mn>
</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the shortest side of this rectangle?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <math alttext="3"><mn>3</mn>
</math>. The area of a rectangle can be calculated by multiplying the length of its longest side by the length of its shortest side. It&rsquo;s given that the area of the rectangle is <math alttext="57"><mn>57</mn>
</math> square inches and the length of the longest side of the rectangle is <math alttext="19"><mn>19</mn>
</math> inches. Let <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> represent the length, in inches, of the shortest side of this rectangle. It follows that <math alttext="57 equals 19 x"><mn>57</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="19"><mn>19</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="3 equals x"><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></math>. Therefore, the length, in inches, of the shortest side of the rectangle is <math alttext="3"><mn>3</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4efea6a3","external_id":"2bde9f21-d49c-42af-b7ec-38c87b242d02","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"c0652b03-9db3-4311-a9fc-75f008693976","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The area of a rectangle is <math alttext=\"57\"><mn>57</mn>\n</math> square inches. The length of the longest side of the rectangle is <math alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn>\n</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the shortest side of this rectangle?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["3"],"correct_answer":["3"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math>. The area of a rectangle can be calculated by multiplying the length of its longest side by the length of its shortest side. It&rsquo;s given that the area of the rectangle is <math alttext=\"57\"><mn>57</mn>\n</math> square inches and the length of the longest side of the rectangle is <math alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn>\n</math> inches. Let <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> represent the length, in inches, of the shortest side of this rectangle. It follows that <math alttext=\"57 equals 19 x\"><mn>57</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"3 equals x\"><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></math>. Therefore, the length, in inches, of the shortest side of the rectangle is <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357406,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"4efea6a3","skill_cd":"