    ($SATQ$4ff7b652$SATQ$, $SATQ$64da6245-4314-4da9-817e-46dcdd9dc29d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3dafbed8-544f-492f-ab93-c1eba2e36c60$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Lines, angles, and triangles$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Right triangles <math alttext="upper L upper M upper N"><mrow>
	<mi>L</mi>
	<mi>M</mi>
	<mi>N</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper P upper Q upper R"><mrow>
	<mi>P</mi>
	<mi>Q</mi>
	<mi>R</mi>
</mrow>
</math> are similar, where <math alttext="upper L"><mi>L</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper M"><mi>M</mi>
</math> correspond to <math alttext="upper P"><mi>P</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math>, respectively. Angle <math alttext="upper M"><mi>M</mi>
</math> has a measure of <math alttext="53 degree"><mn>53</mn>
<mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure of angle <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"380cc32b-e335-4a2d-acc7-46e3eaa253e8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"37 degree\"><mn>37</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"e351d454-d52b-46a3-89f0-31ada42ce8a4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"32ca2224-8dd2-45b3-8624-cc7992f51e0a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"127 degree\"><mn>127</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"8f831b6d-63da-4e1b-b8e5-5055b6bf847a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"143 degree\"><mn>143</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e351d454-d52b-46a3-89f0-31ada42ce8a4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that triangle <math alttext="upper L upper M upper N"><mrow>
	<mi>L</mi>
	<mi>M</mi>
	<mi>N</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext="upper P upper Q upper R"><mrow>
	<mi>P</mi>
	<mi>Q</mi>
	<mi>R</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. Corresponding angles of similar triangles are congruent. Since angle <math alttext="upper M"><mi>M</mi>
</math> and angle <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math> correspond to each other, they must be congruent. Therefore, if the measure of angle <math alttext="upper M"><mi>M</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="53 degree"><mn>53</mn><mo>°</mo></math>, then the measure of angle <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math> is also&nbsp;<math alttext="53 degree"><mn>53</mn><mo>°</mo></math>.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from concluding that angle <math alttext="upper M"><mi>M</mi>
</math> and angle <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math> are complementary rather than congruent.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from concluding that angle <math alttext="upper M"><mi>M</mi>
</math> and angle <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math> are supplementary rather than congruent.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4ff7b652","external_id":"64da6245-4314-4da9-817e-46dcdd9dc29d","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"3dafbed8-544f-492f-ab93-c1eba2e36c60","uId":"dc2406de-15b4-4152-9a3f-c10992d5a6bc","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Right triangles <math alttext=\"upper L upper M upper N\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>L</mi>\n\t<mi>M</mi>\n\t<mi>N</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>P</mi>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are similar, where <math alttext=\"upper L\"><mi>L</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> correspond to <math alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math>, respectively. Angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> has a measure of <math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"380cc32b-e335-4a2d-acc7-46e3eaa253e8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"37 degree\"><mn>37</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"e351d454-d52b-46a3-89f0-31ada42ce8a4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"32ca2224-8dd2-45b3-8624-cc7992f51e0a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"127 degree\"><mn>127</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"8f831b6d-63da-4e1b-b8e5-5055b6bf847a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"143 degree\"><mn>143</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"}],"keys":["e351d454-d52b-46a3-89f0-31ada42ce8a4"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that triangle <math alttext=\"upper L upper M upper N\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>L</mi>\n\t<mi>M</mi>\n\t<mi>N</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>P</mi>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Corresponding angles of similar triangles are congruent. Since angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> correspond to each other, they must be congruent. Therefore, if the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn><mo>°</mo></math>, then the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> is also&nbsp;<math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn><mo>°</mo></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from concluding that angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> are complementary rather than congruent.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from concluding that angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> are supplementary rather than congruent.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959837,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"4ff7b652","skill_cd":"S.B.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","createDate":1691007959837,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":null,"external_id":"64da6245-4314-4da9-817e-46dcdd9dc29d","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"dc2406de-15b4-4152-9a3f-c10992d5a6bc","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["e351d454-d52b-46a3-89f0-31ada42ce8a4"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that triangle <math alttext=\"upper L upper M upper N\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>L</mi>\n\t<mi>M</mi>\n\t<mi>N</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>P</mi>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Corresponding angles of similar triangles are congruent. Since angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> correspond to each other, they must be congruent. Therefore, if the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn><mo>°</mo></math>, then the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> is also&nbsp;<math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn><mo>°</mo></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from concluding that angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> are complementary rather than congruent.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from concluding that angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math> are supplementary rather than congruent.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Right triangles <math alttext=\"upper L upper M upper N\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>L</mi>\n\t<mi>M</mi>\n\t<mi>N</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>P</mi>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are similar, where <math alttext=\"upper L\"><mi>L</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> correspond to <math alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math>, respectively. Angle <math alttext=\"upper M\"><mi>M</mi>\n</math> has a measure of <math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi>\n</math>?</p>","externalid":"64da6245-4314-4da9-817e-46dcdd9dc29d","templateid":"fc9e09b9-b7cf-404c-ba35-6c0e79be05c7","vaultid":"3dafbed8-544f-492f-ab93-c1eba2e36c60","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"380cc32b-e335-4a2d-acc7-46e3eaa253e8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"37 degree\"><mn>37</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"e351d454-d52b-46a3-89f0-31ada42ce8a4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"53 degree\"><mn>53</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"32ca2224-8dd2-45b3-8624-cc7992f51e0a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"127 degree\"><mn>127</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"8f831b6d-63da-4e1b-b8e5-5055b6bf847a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"143 degree\"><mn>143</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1691007959837,"updateDate":1691007959837}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959837, 1691007959837),
    ($SATQ$5011b039$SATQ$, $SATQ$0d7ac734-4775-44b6-bcf4-405ac5ab2009$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Circles$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">What is the radius of the circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane defined by <math alttext="left parenthesis x plus 2 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y plus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 169"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>? &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["13"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["13"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="13"><mn>13</mn></math>. The equation of a circle in the <em>x</em><em>y</em>-plane can be written in the form <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis x minus h right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus k right parenthesis squared equals r squared"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis h comma k right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></math> is the center of the circle and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the circle. It&rsquo;s given that the circle in the <em>x</em><em>y</em>-plane is defined by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis x plus 2 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y plus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 169"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r squared equals 169"><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r equals 13"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math>. Thus, the radius of the circle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="13"><mn>13</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5011b039","external_id":"0d7ac734-4775-44b6-bcf4-405ac5ab2009","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"48a95072-4f26-43f0-b2aa-e5fcd2c13f87","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.D.","skill_desc":"Circles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">What is the radius of the circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane defined by <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x plus 2 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y plus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 169\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>? &nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["13"],"correct_answer":["13"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn></math>. The equation of a circle in the <em>x</em><em>y</em>-plane can be written in the form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x minus h right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus k right parenthesis squared equals r squared\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis h comma k right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></math> is the center of the circle and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the circle. It&rsquo;s given that the circle in the <em>x</em><em>y</em>-plane is defined by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x plus 2 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y plus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 169\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r squared equals 169\"><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r equals 13\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math>. Thus, the radius of the circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1776362727991,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"5011b039","skill_cd":"S.D.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Circles","createDate":1776362727991,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"0d7ac734-4775-44b6-bcf4-405ac5ab2009","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"48a95072-4f26-43f0-b2aa-e5fcd2c13f87","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">What is the radius of the circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane defined by <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x plus 2 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y plus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 169\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>? &nbsp;</p>","keys":["13"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn></math>. The equation of a circle in the <em>x</em><em>y</em>-plane can be written in the form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x minus h right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus k right parenthesis squared equals r squared\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis h comma k right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></math> is the center of the circle and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the circle. It&rsquo;s given that the circle in the <em>x</em><em>y</em>-plane is defined by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x plus 2 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y plus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 169\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r squared equals 169\"><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r equals 13\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math>. Thus, the radius of the circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"0d7ac734-4775-44b6-bcf4-405ac5ab2009","correct_answer":["13"]},"createDate":1776362727991,"updateDate":1776362727991}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727991, 1776362727991),
    ($SATQ$502d9690$SATQ$, $SATQ$a5e0e74e-362f-4f21-aade-6a5d1b2518e0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Rectangle <em><math alttext="italic ABCD"><mtext mathvariant="italic">ABCD</mtext></math></em><em>&nbsp;</em>is similar to rectangle <em><math alttext="italic EFGH"><mtext mathvariant="italic">EFGH</mtext></math></em>. The area of rectangle <em><math alttext="italic ABCD"><mtext mathvariant="italic">ABCD</mtext></math> </em>is <math alttext="648"><mn>648</mn>
</math> square inches, and the area of rectangle <em><math alttext="italic EFGH"><mtext mathvariant="italic">EFGH</mtext></math> </em>is <math alttext="72"><mn>72</mn>
</math> square inches. The length of the longest side of rectangle <em><math alttext="italic ABCD"><mtext mathvariant="italic">ABCD</mtext></math> </em>is <math alttext="36"><mn>36</mn>
</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the longest side of rectangle <em><math alttext="italic EFGH"><mtext mathvariant="italic">EFGH</mtext></math></em>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a72da469-dbfb-4911-bd4e-81994efec7fe","content":"<p><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"5d2c965a-46ae-4b33-80c8-6cfc2f583710","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"7b28d9a8-8e6b-4064-887b-fde6a5e73cfa","content":"<p><math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"a4ff4ee3-8b9f-4ca4-ae68-781c7934b8f9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"36\"><mn>36</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7b28d9a8-8e6b-4064-887b-fde6a5e73cfa"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is correct. It's given that rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is similar to rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>. Therefore, if the length of each side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k"><mi>k</mi></math> times the length of the corresponding side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, then the area of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k squared"><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> times the area of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the area of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="648"><mn>648</mn></math> square inches and the area of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="72"><mn>72</mn></math> square inches. It follows that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k squared equals StartFraction 648 Over 72 EndFraction"><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>648</mn><mn>72</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k squared equals 9"><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math>. Taking the square root of each side of this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k equals StartRoot 9 EndRoot"><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>9</mn></msqrt></math>,&nbsp;or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k equals 3"><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>. It follows that the length of each side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3"><mn>3</mn></math> times the length of the corresponding side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the length of the longest side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="36"><mn>36</mn></math> inches. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="36"><mn>36</mn></math> inches is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3"><mn>3</mn></math> times the length of the longest side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, and the longest side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> is equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 36 Over 3 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>36</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="12"><mn>12</mn></math>, inches.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of the longest side of a rectangle with side lengths that are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="one ninth"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math> the corresponding side lengths of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math>, rather than a rectangle with an area that is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="one ninth"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math> the area of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the factor by which the area of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is larger than the area of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, not the length, in inches, of the longest side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of the longest side of rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math>, not rectangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E upper F upper G upper H"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"502d9690","external_id":"a5e0e74e-362f-4f21-aade-6a5d1b2518e0","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"3fee9ad6-749b-49aa-842c-1ab05b80e786","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>Rectangle <em><math alttext=\"italic ABCD\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">ABCD</mtext></math></em><em>&nbsp;</em>is similar to rectangle <em><math alttext=\"italic EFGH\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">EFGH</mtext></math></em>. The area of rectangle <em><math alttext=\"italic ABCD\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">ABCD</mtext></math> </em>is <math alttext=\"648\"><mn>648</mn>\n</math> square inches, and the area of rectangle <em><math alttext=\"italic EFGH\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">EFGH</mtext></math> </em>is <math alttext=\"72\"><mn>72</mn>\n</math> square inches. The length of the longest side of rectangle <em><math alttext=\"italic ABCD\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">ABCD</mtext></math> </em>is <math alttext=\"36\"><mn>36</mn>\n</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the longest side of rectangle <em><math alttext=\"italic EFGH\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">EFGH</mtext></math></em>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a72da469-dbfb-4911-bd4e-81994efec7fe","content":"<p><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"5d2c965a-46ae-4b33-80c8-6cfc2f583710","con